  "live_records": {
    "q-140480": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140480",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=4-4+4=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0+3-3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0-3+3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
