    "q-83718": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83718",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-1+0-4-2=-7\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2+0-2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=-18+18=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
