    "q-39396": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39396",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$\n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=6-16+10=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=-8+10=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=15-12-1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\".",
