    "q-39490": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39490",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=1+6-1-6=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2-2+2-2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2-3-2+3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2-4+2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]=-12+0+12=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]=-6+0+6=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=-8+2=-6\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
